/// The suites cargo has to announce, as it announces them.
fn suites() -> Vec<String> {
    // One entry per *crate*, keeping the duplicates: two crates holding a
    // `properties.rs` means the name has to be announced twice.
    SUITES
        .iter()
        .map(|(_, file)| format!("tests/{file}"))
        .collect()
}

/// Rustdoc over every integration test target, because `cargo doc` skips them.
///
/// **The step above documents lib and bin targets and nothing else.** Its own
/// comment says nothing else asks whether a doc link resolves outside
/// `tests/` — and every file under `tests/`, which is where the gate suite's
/// outer layer lives, is outside
/// that step too.
/// Measured, a link to a name that exists nowhere planted in
/// `crates/nc-journal/tests/tampering.rs`:
///
/// ```text
/// cargo doc --workspace --no-deps --document-private-items      EXIT=0
/// cargo rustdoc -p nc-journal --test tampering
///   error: unresolved link to `no_such_item_anywhere`
/// ```
///
/// One invocation per target, because `cargo rustdoc` takes one at a time. The
/// list is [`SUITES`] rather than a second walk of `tests/`: that list is
/// already what the test verdict is measured against and already has a walk
/// under it, and a private list here would be a second thing to keep in step.
///
/// Each package depends on itself under `[dev-dependencies]` so this can work
/// at all — cargo passes no `--extern` for the crate under test when it
/// documents a test target, so a `use nc_ids::…` at the top of a test file is
/// an unresolved import to rustdoc. Only a `use` fails: a fully-qualified call
/// inside a function body resolves, because rustdoc does not read bodies — so a
/// test file that documents cleanly without the self-dependency is no proof
/// that the rest would.
fn the_test_targets_are_documented_too(root: &Path, offline: bool, failures: &mut Vec<String>) {
    let mut documented = 0_usize;
    let mut broken: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
    for (package, file) in SUITES {
        let target = file.trim_end_matches(".rs");
        match cargo(
            root,
            &["rustdoc", "-q", "-p", package, "--test", target, "--locked"],
            offline,
        ) {
            Ok(_) => documented += 1,
            Err(output) => {
                broken.push(format!("{package} tests/{file}"));
                ui::failure(&format!("documentation of {package} tests/{file}"));
                for line in output.lines() {
                    ui::detail(line);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if !broken.is_empty() {
        failures.push("a test target does not document".to_owned());
        return;
    }
    // The count is the assertion, the same way the suite verdict's is: a list
    // that emptied would report nothing broken because it looked at nothing.
    if documented < SUITES.len() {
        ui::failure(&format!(
            "{documented} test target(s) were documented and this tree has {}",
            SUITES.len()
        ));
        failures.push("the test-target documentation walk is short".to_owned());
        return;
    }
    ui::ok(&format!("documentation of {documented} test target(s)"));
}

/// Every sentence the gate can say, in the order they sort.
///
/// **Twenty-six guards deleted in one edit left the run identical, with no
/// list like this one.** Measured: sixteen floors and error reports removed
/// from `check.rs`, two enumerate-versus-judge floors from `gates.rs` and three
/// branches from `workflow.rs`, each condition preserved as `let _ = …;` so
/// nothing else moved, and `cargo xtask check` printed the same five lines, the
/// same `849 test(s)`, and `check passed`. A guard with no test of its own is
/// held by nothing but this list, and a floor under a walk — the mechanism this
/// repository relies on most — is the kind a run cannot show missing. One of
/// the twenty-six, the edited-migration report, is the only reader of
/// `_sqlx_migrations` for that case, and no step compares the tree with what it
/// held before: with it gone, a migration edited after it was applied passed
/// the gate.
///
/// A verdict name is what `check failed: …` prints and a finding's `what` is
/// the line a developer reads, so between them this list is everything the gate
/// is able to say. Deleting a guard removes one — sixteen of the twenty-six
/// were verdicts and the rest reported through a `Finding`, which is why both
/// populations are here and why a list of only the first would have covered
/// less than two thirds of them. It is
/// compared **both** ways and as a multiset rather than a set: five names are
/// pushed from two places each, so a set would report a pair as intact after
/// one of them went, and a one-way comparison would let a new guard arrive
/// unpinned — which is how a list stops being a record of what is there.
///
/// This does not say a guard is *right*; `xtask/src/corpus.rs` and
/// `xtask/mutants.py` are where a predicate's verdict is decided. It says the
/// guard is still there, which is the question a deletion asks.
///
/// **A `static`, because this list is past what clippy will inline as a
/// constant**, as `OPERATORS` is: one copy rather than one per use, and nothing
/// reads it at compile time.
