/// The verdicts the source is able to push, read out of `xtask/src/`.
///
/// The directory is walked rather than named file by file, so a verdict pushed
/// from a file added later is in this and a file deleted takes its verdicts
/// with it. The floor under the walk is the file count: a walk that read
/// nothing would report every pinned verdict as gone, which is loud, and a walk
/// that read half would report half of them as gone, which is also loud — but a
/// walk that read none *and* a list that emptied would agree, so the count is
/// checked against the directory rather than against the result.
#[cfg(test)]
fn the_verdicts_the_source_can_push() -> Vec<String> {
    // Held apart from their uses below so each needle is written once. Written
    // twice, the first is also *found* twice, by itself.
    // **Any receiver, not `failures` alone.** Measured with a branch-coverage
    // census: 395 cold branch bodies and thirty guards that could be deleted
    // with the console unchanged, most of them invisible to a list that names
    // one receiver. The same sentences are pushed into `wrong`,
    // `problems`, `drifted`, `missing`, `unhealthy`, `outside`, `caveats`,
    // `strangers`, `broken`, `renamed`, `lost` and `expected`, thirty-eight of
    // them, and every one is a thing the gate can say. The receiver is a local
    // variable's name; it is not the rule.
    const THE_VERDICT: &str = ".push(";
    // **And `.extend(`, which carries a verdict as surely as `.push(` does.**
    //
    // **It is a spelling this tree writes.** `the_journal_is_still_append_only`
    // extends `wrong` with a `format!`, carrying one of acceptance criterion 3's
    // two tamper verdicts — and without this needle both can be reworded to say
    // the database agrees, with `check passed` and every test green. The
    // receiver rule applies unchanged: a receiver is a local variable's
    // name and is not the rule.
    const THE_VERDICTS: &str = ".extend(";
    //
    // **What reading more of it costs, measured.** Read by a reader that ran
    // past the call it was handed, six things the gate cannot say entered the
    // record — `", "`, `"bans"`, and a fragment of this file's own source.
    // Narrowed to an array of literals written at the call, the remaining six
    // were `["AGENTS.md", "CLAUDE.md", "README.md"]` and
    // `["bans", "licenses", "sources"]`: every `.extend(` this tree writes with
    // a literal array carries *values*, and no filter tells a file name from a
    // sentence. So this needle is read exactly as `.push(` is, and an array
    // written at the call is not an argument that reading takes.

    // And the line under a verdict, which is where a gate says what to do about
    // it. `ui::detail` is how the store, the stack and the policy steps hand a
    // developer the command that fixes what just failed.
    const THE_DETAIL: &str = "ui::detail(";
    // **Assembled, so that writing it here does not make this file say it.**
    // Spelled out, the needle matches its own definition. And it is the bare
    // struct literal, not `findings.push(Finding {`, which names one receiver
    // out of several: ten guards push a `Finding` built inside a helper
    // (`fn hidden(…) -> Finding`, `fn unbounded`, `fn runs_nothing`, `fn missing`,
    // `fn finding`) or into a differently-named vector (`found.push`,
    // `announced.push`, `written_paths.push`, `Ok(vec![Finding {`), and every one
    // of them is a sentence the gate can say. One is `runs_nothing`, the only
    // reader for a migration emptied after it was applied: measured with the
    // narrower needle, deleted whole — call site, function and doc — the
    // console was identical and `860 test(s)` unchanged.
    const THE_FINDING: &str = concat!("Finding", " {");
    // **And the two ways a verdict travels that are neither.** `finish`'s
    // `--offline` equality — the only reader of `OFFLINE_SKIPS`, the constant
    // four documents quote — calls `ui::failure` and then returns `Err`, so
    // without this needle it is outside this census entirely: measured, deleted
    // in the `let _ = …` shape, the console identical and `865 test(s)`
    // unchanged.
    // A guard whose verdict travels by `Err` return rather than by a push is
    // still a sentence the gate can say.
    const THE_REPORT: &str = "ui::failure(";
    const THE_RETURN: &str = "Err(";
    // **And the two ways a `Result` is turned into one without the word.**
    // `THE_RETURN` names one spelling of a returned verdict;
    // `.map_err(|error| format!("…"))` and `.ok_or_else(|| format!("…"))` are the
    // same journey with no `Err(` anywhere. Measured without this needle: 85
    // literal-carrying sites in `xtask/src`, **56 distinct sentences** in no
    // row of the record and every one of them in `strings target/debug/xtask` —
    // including the verdict `sections.rs` returns when no workspace is found
    // above the tree, which is the condition that makes the citation gate
    // silently weaker, and which can then be rewritten to say the tree is in
    // perfect order with every test silent.
    const THE_MAPPED: &str = ".map_err(";
    const THE_MISSING: &str = ".ok_or_else(";
    // **And a skip, which is a step saying it did not run.** `ui::warn` is free
    // in unlimited quantity: `finish` allows a `skipped:` line without asking
    // what it says, so a `return` behind a new one turns any step off and exits
    // 0. The `--offline` count bounds the skips the flag names and nothing
    // bounds another reason. Pinned here, adding a skip is the same two-place
    // edit as adding a guard — and the second place is a list a reviewer reads.
    const THE_SKIP: &str = "ui::warn(";
    // **And a part saying it does not apply on this system.** `finish` holds
    // the parts a run prints to the list written for the system, and this holds
    // every sentence such a line can carry, so a new one is the same two-place
    // edit as a new skip.
    const THE_ASIDE: &str = "ui::not_applicable(";
    let directory = std::path::Path::new(concat!(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR"), "/src"));
    // **`walk`, not `read_dir`.** `ui.rs`'s own scanner holds the same rule: a
    // module written as a directory of its own, declared with a `mod` line and
    // living one level down, sits outside a one-level listing — and every
    // verdict it pushes would be outside this census. Measured with
    // `read_dir` and such a file holding a `ui::failure` and a `failures.push`,
    // both of sentences nobody wrote down: this test green and the whole suite
    // byte-identical to baseline.
    let mut files: Vec<std::path::PathBuf> = Vec::new();
    crate::gates::walk(directory, &mut |path| {
        if path.extension().is_some_and(|kind| kind == "rs") {
            files.push(path.to_path_buf());
        }
    })
    .expect("this crate has a source directory");
    files.sort();
    assert!(
        files.len() >= 15,
        "the walk found {} source file(s) in {}, so it is not reading this crate",
        files.len(),
        directory.display()
    );
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    for path in files {
        // **The code, not the comments.** A `Finding` written out in prose —
        // this file does it twice, explaining the rule — is not a sentence the
        // gate can say, and counting it puts an entry in the list that no guard
        // produces.
        let text = crate::gates::without_comments(
            &std::fs::read_to_string(&path).expect("a source file is readable"),
            "//",
        );
        for (needle, after) in [
            (THE_VERDICT, ""),
            (THE_VERDICTS, ""),
            (THE_FINDING, "what:"),
            // **The remedy is not read, and that is a written gap.**
            // `ui::detail(&finding.fix)` prints it under every verdict — the
            // same `ui::detail` this list names as *where a gate says what to do
            // about it* — but the argument is a field rather than a literal, so
            // none of the 91 `fix:` lines in `xtask/src` is in the record.
            // Measured: a shipped remedy reworded to nonsense, and another
            // deleted outright to `String::new()`, both left `check passed`,
            // `445 test(s)` and this census green. Half of every finding is
            // unpinned.
            //
            // Reading them was measured too, and it does not land as it stands:
            // one remedy quotes an example migration filename and carries its
            // own reference-hatch marker for the *document* it is printed into.
            // Pulled into this file as a literal it becomes a citation the
            // reference gate reads, and the marker that excuses it there is
            // counted but not applied here — so adding the needle trades a
            // written gap for a gate arguing with itself. What this needs is for
            // the reference gate and this one to agree about a remedy's example
            // filename, and that is a change to two gates rather than a needle.
            // The marker and the filename are deliberately not spelled out in
            // this paragraph: a census counts a hatch wherever it is written and
            // the reference gate reads a path the same way.
            //
            // `every_finding_carries_a_remedy_that_is_not_the_defect_restated`
            // is the only test over the field and it drives one finding from one
            // gate, so it is not a substitute either.
            (THE_REPORT, ""),
            (THE_RETURN, ""),
            (THE_MAPPED, ""),
            (THE_MISSING, ""),
            (THE_SKIP, ""),
            (THE_ASIDE, ""),
            (THE_DETAIL, ""),
        ] {
            found.extend(the_sentences_after(&text, needle, after));
        }
        // **And the floor helper, whose verdict is its argument.** Three
        // sentences reach a developer only through `inspected_something`, which
        // returns `Err(named.to_owned())` — so the sentence is at the call site,
        // held in a `const` and passed by name, and no needle above can see it.
        // They are `CITATIONS`, `STALE` and `DRIFT`: each says a whole step
        // inspected nothing, which is the loudest thing this gate says and the
        // one it could not account for.
        found.extend(the_floors_named_in(&text));
    }
    found.sort();
    found
}
#[cfg(test)]
/// The sentences a local function hands back, when a verdict travels by a call.
///
/// **A verdict is not always a literal and not always a name.**
/// `crate::ui::warn(&announcement(&verdict, first))` is a step saying it did
/// not run, and the three sentences it can say are written one match arm deep
/// in a helper beside it — so the literal is nowhere near the call and there is
/// no binding to resolve. Unread, all three of `wall.rs`'s
/// `skipped: the hollow build …` lines are printable by this gate and appear in
/// neither the census nor the list; measured, rewriting one to a sentence nobody
/// had written leaves `everything_the_gate_can_say_is_written_down ... ok`.
///
/// Only a literal in *answer position* is read — after a match arm's `=>`,
/// after `return`, or as the argument of a `format!` or a `concat!` in one of
/// those places. A message inside `unreachable!` or `expect` is what happens
/// when the program is already wrong, which is not something the gate says.
fn the_sentences_returned_by(name: &str, text: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    let Some(at) = text.find(&format!("fn {name}(")) else {
        return found;
    };
    let Some(opens) = text[at..].find(" {\n").map(|offset| at + offset + 2) else {
        return found;
    };
    let body = &text[opens..];
    let body = &body[..body.find("\n}\n").map_or(body.len(), |end| end + 1)];
    // **From the answer, not from the literal.** Asking what stands
    // immediately before a quote accepts every literal in the body, because a
    // wrapped `format!(` leaves the literal alone on the next line — measured,
    // that read a hundred and eighty fragments out of the helpers this gate
    // calls and reported them as verdicts. So the walk starts at each place an
    // answer is given and reads forward, which is the rule the scan above the
    // call sites already uses.
    // **And a body that *is* a choice**, which is the most ordinary way to
    // write a two-answer helper in Rust and which the anchors below cannot see:
    // they look for `=>` and `return `, and a tail `if`/`else` has neither.
    // Measured, `ledger_difference` — the only reader that tells a reworded
    // comment from an unreserved error code — both of whose sentences were
    // pinned by nothing and could be rewritten with `434 passed`.
    // The tail is the last statement that opens at the function's own
    // indentation; anything before it is bound to a name or returned, and both
    // are read by the anchors below.
    // **At the body's own indentation, not at four spaces.** Needles written
    // `"\n    if "` and `"\n    match "` find a top-level `fn` in a top-level
    // module and nothing else: a helper inside a nested `mod` is indented
    // eight, and its tail choice would go unread. The indentation that
    // matters is the one this body actually uses, so it is measured from the
    // body's first indented line rather than assumed.
    let indent = body
        .lines()
        .find(|line| !line.trim().is_empty() && line.starts_with(' '))
        .map_or(4, |line| line.len() - line.trim_start().len());
    let opens_if = format!("\n{}if ", " ".repeat(indent));
    let opens_match = format!("\n{}match ", " ".repeat(indent));
    let tail = [opens_if.as_str(), opens_match.as_str()]
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|opens| body.rfind(opens))
        .max();
    if let Some(at) = tail {
        found.extend(the_clauses_of(&body[at..]));
    }
    for anchor in ["=>", "return "] {
        let mut from = 0_usize;
        while let Some(offset) = body[from..].find(anchor) {
            let after = from + offset + anchor.len();
            from = after;
            let Some(quote) = body[after..].find('"').map(|offset| after + offset) else {
                break;
            };
            let between: String = body[after..quote]
                .chars()
                .filter(|character| !character.is_whitespace())
                .collect();
            if !matches!(between.as_str(), "" | "format!(" | "concat!(") {
                continue;
            }
            let (sentence, _) = if between == "concat!(" {
                the_concatenation_at(&body[quote..], None)
            } else {
                the_literal_at(&body[quote..])
            };
            found.push(sentence);
        }
    }
    found.sort();
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Whether the *expected* half of a two-count floor is one this rule can leave
/// alone.
///
/// A floor with two numbers fires on a difference, so the second is as
/// load-bearing as the first, and a rule that reads only the count leaves it
/// free. Measured, `inspected_all(judged, manifests.len().min(1), COLOURS)` is
/// one token and turns that floor into `judged > 0`, with the console
/// byte-identical; and the colour gate's own comment records what the step
/// printed when its number was the listing rather than what it judged:
/// `ok 10 manifest(s) reach the colour system` over a walk that read none.
///
/// It is a slightly wider question than the count's, but only slightly. An
/// expectation is legitimately *computed* — `members_of(root)`,
/// `how_many_paragraphs(root)`, or the wider of two counts — which does not make
/// it shapeless, and a deny-list in place of a shape is one every new spelling
/// walks through. The shape is
/// the count's own, with two differences: a number written out is refused,
/// because an expectation of zero is a floor that cannot fire; and a `min` or a
/// `max` between two counts is allowed, because neither side of it is a
/// number.
fn is_a_plain_expectation(expression: &str) -> bool {
    let written = expression.trim();
    if a_number_written_out(written) {
        return false;
    }
    // **Not a deny-list, because one has no last entry.** Three spellings of
    // *make this small* — `.min(1)`, `take(1)`, `.first().iter().count()` — are
    // joined by eight more, measured walking straight through such a list:
    // `usize::from(!x.is_empty())`, `x.is_empty() as
    // usize`, `x.len().signum()`, `x.iter().step_by(x.len()).count()`,
    // `x.len().saturating_sub(x.len() - 1)`, a `const` that is 1, a function
    // returning 1, and `x.len().min(ONE)`. Each is exactly 1 for any non-empty
    // walk, holds no clamp and no literal, and turns a two-number floor into
    // `count > 0` with the console byte-identical.
    //
    // There is a closed shape to invert into, and it is the one beside it: an
    // expectation is a count of
    // what the tree holds, which is what [`is_a_plain_count`] already spells
    // out as a name, its fields and a reader from a closed list. `members_of(
    // root)` and `how_many_paragraphs(root)` — the computed expectations this
    // gate actually ships — are that shape; every one of the eleven spellings
    // above is not, because `min`, `signum`, `step_by`, `saturating_sub`,
    // `take`, `first`, `from` and `is_empty` are none of them readers.
    //
    // What this adds over the count's own rule is the literal: a count of zero
    // is what a floor is written to notice, and an *expectation* of zero is a
    // floor that cannot.
    if is_a_plain_count(written) {
        return true;
    }
    // **And the wider of two counts is still a count.** `applied.len().max(
    // sql_files(&on_disk))` is one this gate ships: the migrations applied
    // against the files on disk, whichever is larger, and neither side is a
    // number. What must not stand on either side is a number *or a name for
    // one* — `x.len().min(1)` is the plainest spelling this rule refuses and
    // `x.len().min(ONE)` is the same clamp with the number written down
    // elsewhere. A constant compared against a count is a clamp whatever it
    // holds, which is why this refuses it here and leaves a constant standing
    // *alone* to the caller, where the name is followed to its binding: alone
    // it is a ratchet, and `sites` against `FLOORS` is how half this file is
    // written.
    if let Some((left, right)) = the_wider_of(written) {
        return !a_written_constant(left)
            && !a_written_constant(right)
            && is_a_plain_expectation(left)
            && is_a_plain_expectation(right);
    }
    false
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// A number written out, suffix and all.
///
/// As a whole expectation it is a pinned floor, and `0` is one of them:
/// `inspected_all(n, 0)` is `n > 0`. It is also what a name has to be followed
/// to its binding to see, since `const ONE: usize = 1;` handed over as a name
/// reads as an ordinary count.
fn a_number_written_out(expression: &str) -> bool {
    let written = expression.trim();
    written.starts_with(|character: char| character.is_ascii_digit())
        && written
            .chars()
            .all(|character| character.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || character == '_')
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// A name written in this tree's spelling for a constant.
fn a_written_constant(expression: &str) -> bool {
    let written = expression.trim();
    !written.is_empty()
        && written
            .chars()
            .all(|character| character.is_ascii_uppercase() || character == '_')
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The two sides of a `.max(…)` or `.min(…)` that is the whole expression.
///
/// Returns nothing when the call is in the middle of a chain, because then it is
/// not what the expression *is* — the reader that follows it decides that, and
/// the closed shape above reads the chain.
fn the_wider_of(expression: &str) -> Option<(&str, &str)> {
    let written = expression.trim();
    for name in [".max(", ".min("] {
        let mut at = 0_usize;
        while let Some(offset) = written[at..].find(name) {
            let open = at + offset + name.len();
            at = open;
            let Some(end) = the_matching_bracket_of(&written[open..]) else {
                continue;
            };
            if written[open + end + 1..].trim().is_empty() {
                return Some((&written[..open - name.len()], &written[open..open + end]));
            }
        }
    }
    None
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Whether an expression is a plain count, and so a thing that can be zero.
///
/// **Written the other way round, this rule is vacuous.** A list of the
/// operations that keep a count above zero — `max`, `clamp`, `saturating_add`,
/// `unwrap_or` with a non-zero default, a literal `1` added on either side — can
/// have its whole body replaced with `false`, answering *no floor is lifted* for
/// every site, with the suite green and nothing in the tree to say so. A list
/// of six also leaves `count | 1`, `count + 2`,
/// `count.next_power_of_two()`, `.unwrap_or_else(|| 1)` and
/// `if count == 0 { 1 } else { count }` — five more spellings of one idea, and
/// there is no last one.
///
/// So the question is inverted, which is what closes the class. A floor exists to
/// notice a zero, and the twenty-five counts this tree hands to one are all the
/// same shape: a name, a field path, a counter's own `0_usize`, or a call ending
/// in `len`, `count` or `sum`. Anything else is arithmetic between the counting
/// and the floor, and this rule does not try to decide what that arithmetic
/// does — it says there is some.
///
/// **What it still refuses that it should not, measured rather than guessed.**
/// A closed shape is refused-by-default, so every spelling nobody has written
/// yet is refused, and these four are measured:
///
/// - `walked.iter().skip(1).count()` and `walked.iter().zip(other).count()` —
///   both can only shrink a walk, but both take an argument that is not a
///   closure, and *which readers may be handed a value* would be a second list
///   beside `READING`. `chain` is the reason there is no blanket allowance:
///   `iter().chain(once(x)).count()` is at least one.
/// - `compiled.ledger.reserved_lines()` and `report.total()` — a method that
///   produces a count. `reserved_lines(&compiled.ledger.contents)` is this
///   rule's own accepted example and the identical function spelled as a method
///   is refused, so the verdict turns on where the call is written. Accepting
///   any terminal method would re-admit `signum()`, `is_some()` and
///   `next_power_of_two()`, which is the class the inversion closed.
/// - `counts[at]` and any other indexing.
///
/// All four fail *safe*: the rule refuses a floor rather than admitting a bad
/// one, and the cost is a diagnostic on correct code the day somebody writes
/// one. That is the trade this shape makes, and it is written down here so the
/// next person to hit it knows it was measured and not overlooked.
fn is_a_plain_count(expression: &str) -> bool {
    /// The whole of what a counting expression may be made of.
    ///
    /// **A closed set, because asking what an expression *ends in* is a list of
    /// spellings again.** `(0..=n).count()` is `n + 1` — arithmetic between the
    /// counting and the floor, which is what this rule says it refuses — and it
    /// ends in `count`, so a rule asking what an expression ends in accepts it and
    /// the drift floor can never fire.
    /// `once(x).chain(it).count()` and `[n].len()` are the same trick.
    ///
    /// **And every ordinary reader.** `filter_map` and `flat_map` beside `filter`
    /// and `flatten`, and `rev`, `enumerate` and `bytes`: each is an ordinary way
    /// to write a count of what the tree holds — measured without them,
    /// `declared.iter().filter_map(|e| e.name()).count()` and
    /// `text.bytes().count()` are both refused — and a rule that refuses a
    /// legitimate count is green only until somebody writes one.
    const READING: [&str; 24] = [
        "iter",
        "split",
        "map_or",
        "take_while",
        "collect",
        "into_iter",
        "values",
        "keys",
        "filter",
        "filter_map",
        "flatten",
        "flat_map",
        "map",
        "copied",
        "cloned",
        "chars",
        "bytes",
        "lines",
        "rev",
        "enumerate",
        "split_whitespace",
        "len",
        "count",
        "sum",
    ];

    /// Whether a call's arguments hold a constant, zero aside.
    ///
    /// A count is a thing that can be zero, and a constant reaching whatever
    /// produces it is the shape that stops it being one — the same shape whether
    /// it is written `take(1)`, `nth(0)`, `max(read, 1)` or `usize::from(true)`.
    ///
    /// The caller hands this text whose string contents are already blanked, so
    /// a digit inside a literal is not a number reaching a call.
    fn holds_a_constant(arguments: &str) -> bool {
        arguments
            .split(|character: char| !(character.is_alphanumeric() || character == '_'))
            .any(|token| {
                token == "true"
                    || token == "false"
                    || (token.starts_with(|character: char| character.is_ascii_digit())
                        && token
                            .chars()
                            .any(|character| ('1'..='9').contains(&character)))
            })
    }

    // A name, then nothing but fields of it and readers of those. The receiver
    // has to be a name: a range, an array literal and a free call are each a way
    // to write a number that cannot be zero, and none of them is a thing this
    // tree counts.
    let written = expression.trim();
    // **And two counts added are a count.** `declared.len() + extra.len()` is
    // zero when both are, and refusing it would be this rule being too strong —
    // the shape it exists to catch is a *number* on one side, which is why
    // `1 + read` and `read + 2` are in its own refused list and stay there: each
    // side has to be a count in its own right.
    if let Some((left, right)) = written.split_once(" + ") {
        return is_a_plain_count(left) && is_a_plain_count(right);
    }
    // **A count that cannot be zero is not a floor.** `split` yields one piece
    // for the empty text and one more for every separator in it, so a split
    // counted directly is at least 1 whatever it is handed — measured with
    // rustc, `"".split("\n\n").count()` is 1 where `"".lines().count()` is 0.
    // The rule this predicate serves is called
    // `no_floor_is_handed_a_count_that_cannot_be_zero`, and without this a floor
    // written that way is read as ordinary and can never fire.
    //
    // What this must not refuse is a split that can yield nothing, which is any
    // split with an adapter between it and the count: `filter`, `take_while`
    // and `flat_map` all drop, and each is an ordinary count of what a document
    // holds.
    for reader in ["split", "splitn", "rsplit", "split_terminator"] {
        for (at, _) in written.match_indices(reader) {
            let after = &written[at + reader.len()..];
            if !after.starts_with('(') {
                continue;
            }
            let Some(end) = the_matching_bracket_of(&after[1..]) else {
                continue;
            };
            if after[end + 2..].starts_with(".count()") {
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
    let Some(first) = the_name_at(written) else {
        return false;
    };
    // **A digit is alphanumeric, so `1` would read as a name.** The rule beside
    // this one opens by refusing a literal outright; accepting any of them here
    // lets `inspected_something(1, EXCUSES)` — a floor that can never fire —
    // through with the whole suite green. Zero is the exception and the only
    // one: a counter's own `0_usize` is what a floor is written to notice.
    if first.starts_with(|character: char| character.is_ascii_digit()) {
        // **And the literal has to be the whole expression.** Returning before
        // anything after the literal is looked at would make an expression that
        // merely *begins* with a zero a plain count whatever follows.
        // `0_usize.max(1) + read` is `1 + read`, and `1 + read` is
        // written out in this rule's own refused list; the realistic spelling is
        // the counter's initialiser — `let mut read = 0_usize.max(1);` — after
        // which the floor over it can never fire, and the binding-follow that
        // exists for exactly that would walk straight past.
        return written[first.len()..].trim().is_empty()
            && !first
                .chars()
                .any(|character| ('1'..='9').contains(&character));
    }
    let mut rest = &written[first.len()..];
    // **A path is a name.** `crate::wall::SHAPES.len()` and
    // `nc_catalog::TABLES.len()` count what a module holds, and a walk that
    // stops at the first `::` calls everything after it arithmetic — the shape
    // of a repair that is too strong, green only until a floor is written over
    // one. A turbofish is not a path segment and is read by the loop
    // below, where it belongs to the call.
    let mut rest_of_the_path: Option<&str> = None;
    while let Some(next) = rest.strip_prefix("::") {
        if next.starts_with('<') {
            break;
        }
        let Some(segment) = the_name_at(next) else {
            return false;
        };
        rest_of_the_path = Some(segment);
        rest = &next[segment.len()..];
    }
    // **The receiver may be a call.** `reserved_lines(&compiled.ledger.contents)`
    // returns the thing being counted; what would pin a floor is a *number*
    // handed to it, and that is asked of every call below.
    if let Some(open) = rest.strip_prefix('(') {
        let Some(end) = the_matching_bracket_of(open) else {
            return false;
        };
        if the_name_at(open[..end].trim_start_matches(['&', ' '])).is_none() {
            return false;
        }
        // **And a conversion is not a count.** Asking only that the argument
        // *begin* with a name leaves everything after the first token unread, and
        // an arbitrary boolean becomes a plain count:
        // `usize::from(manifests.iter().any(|manifest| manifest.is_file()))` is
        // 1 for any non-empty walk. Measured at the shipped colour floor with
        // the walk truncated to one manifest of ten:
        // `ok 1 manifest(s) reach the colour system`, and the floor did not fire.
        // `usize::from(manifests.first().is_some())` and
        // `usize::from(manifests.iter().next().is_some())` are the same trick
        // twice more.
        //
        // Refusing the *conversion* rather than reading the argument, because
        // reading it is the wrong question: a receiver call is handed the thing
        // being counted — `read_to_string(root.join(…))` — and what turns
        // something that is not a number into one is `from`, `try_from` and
        // `into`, which are never how this tree counts anything.
        if matches!(first, "from" | "try_from" | "into")
            || rest_of_the_path.is_some_and(|last| matches!(last, "from" | "try_from" | "into"))
        {
            return false;
        }
        // **And no number handed to it.** `max(read, 1)` and
        // `min(files.len(), 1)` are `max` and `min` reached through a `use`, and
        // a refused list holding only `usize::max(read, 1)` and
        // `std::cmp::max(read, 1)` refuses those for holding a `::`, not for
        // being a clamp — a single `use std::cmp::max;` restores the whole
        // spelling. What pins a count is the number,
        // wherever the call it reaches is written down.
        if holds_a_constant(&open[..end]) {
            return false;
        }
        rest = &open[end + 1..];
    }
    the_chain_reads(rest, &READING)
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// What a count expression reads off its receiver, and nothing else.
///
/// Split out of [`is_a_plain_count`], which with it inline is longer than a
/// reader keeps in their head, since the receiver reads paths, conversions and
/// calls. The two halves are two questions: what is being counted, and what is
/// read off it.
fn the_chain_reads(rest: &str, reading: &[&str]) -> bool {
    let mut rest = rest;
    loop {
        // A chain rustfmt has wrapped is the same chain.
        let rested = rest.trim_start();
        if rested.is_empty() {
            return true;
        }
        let Some(after) = rested.strip_prefix('.') else {
            return false;
        };
        let Some(name) = the_name_at(after) else {
            return false;
        };
        // **A turbofish is part of the call, not a field.** `counts.iter().sum()`
        // and `counts.iter().sum::<usize>()` are the same expression, and
        // refusing the second is the shape of a repair that is too strong, green
        // only until somebody writes it.
        // **And a generic holds generics.** Taking the *first* `>` ends
        // `collect::<Vec<_>>()` at the `>` closing `Vec<_`, and the rest —
        // `>().len()` — reads as a field of nothing. Measured: a walk gathered
        // before it is counted is then refused, which is the too-strong
        // direction again.
        let tail = &after[name.len()..];
        let tail = tail
            .strip_prefix("::<")
            .and_then(|generic| {
                let mut depth = 1_i32;
                generic.char_indices().find_map(|(at, character)| {
                    match character {
                        '<' => depth += 1,
                        '>' => depth -= 1,
                        _ => {}
                    }
                    (depth == 0).then_some(&generic[at + 1..])
                })
            })
            .unwrap_or(tail);
        let Some(open) = tail.strip_prefix('(') else {
            // A field of the receiver, which is still the thing being counted.
            rest = tail;
            continue;
        };
        if !reading.contains(&name) {
            return false;
        }
        // A reader takes a closure or nothing. A *number* handed to one is the
        // shape that pins a count, and `take(1)` is how it is written.
        let Some(end) = the_matching_bracket_of(open) else {
            return false;
        };
        let held = open[..end].trim();
        // **A reader takes a closure, nothing, or a text to split on.** A
        // *number* handed to one is the shape that pins a count — `take(1)` is
        // how it is written — and a text is not: `text.split("\n\n").count()`
        // is an ordinary count of what a document holds, and refusing it would
        // be this rule being too strong.
        // **And a reader that defaults to zero may say so.** `map_or(0, …)`
        // reads a file that may not be there and counts what it holds; the
        // default is the *zero* a floor exists to notice, so it is the one
        // number a reader may be handed. Any other is `take(1)` respelled.
        let defaults_to_zero = name == "map_or" && held.starts_with('0');
        if !held.is_empty() && !held.starts_with('|') && !held.starts_with('"') && !defaults_to_zero
        {
            return false;
        }
        // **And a closure that yields a number yields a number.**
        // `manifests.iter().map(|_| 0).sum()` is a spelling of zero, and
        // `|_| 1` is a spelling of the length — neither is a count of anything.
        // The closed shape reads a chain, and this is where it looks inside a
        // closure.
        if let Some(body) = held.rsplit('|').next()
            && a_number_written_out(body)
        {
            return false;
        }
        rest = &open[end + 1..];
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// `text` with its comments and string contents spent, byte for byte.
///
/// [`crate::gates::blanked`] spends a *character* at a time, so a file holding
/// `§`, `—` or `…` — which this one holds by the hundred — comes back shorter in
/// bytes than it went in, and an offset found in one is the wrong place in the
/// other. `the_test_regions` carries a paragraph about exactly that mistake.
/// This keeps the widths, so a needle found here can be read there.
fn the_code_in(text: &str) -> String {
    let spent = crate::gates::blanked(text);
    let mut out = String::with_capacity(text.len());
    for (original, blanked) in text.chars().zip(spent.chars()) {
        if original == blanked {
            out.push(original);
            continue;
        }
        for _ in 0..original.len_utf8() {
            out.push(if blanked == '\n' { '\n' } else { ' ' });
        }
    }
    out
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The identifier at the start of `text`, if it opens with one.
fn the_name_at(text: &str) -> Option<&str> {
    let end = text
        .find(|character: char| !(character.is_alphanumeric() || character == '_'))
        .unwrap_or(text.len());
    (end > 0).then(|| &text[..end])
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The right-hand side a name is bound to, nearest above `before_the_call`.
///
/// The whole statement, not a sentence: what the floor rule asks of it is
/// whether arithmetic keeps it above zero, and that can be written anywhere in
/// the expression.
fn the_expression_bound_to(name: &str, text: &str, before_the_call: usize) -> Option<String> {
    let at = the_binding_of(name, text, before_the_call)?;
    // **And not a binding from another function.** `the_binding_of` takes the
    // nearest `let` above the call wherever it is, which is what the *verdict*
    // reader wants — a sentence is a sentence whoever bound it, and the census
    // that reads it compares whole lists for equality. What reads *this* is the
    // floor rule, and there the question is what the compiler resolves:
    // measured without this, an `expected` three items away resolves to a
    // `PROTECTIONS.iter().map(…).collect()`, so the rule is asked whether a
    // `Vec` can be zero and answers about a collection nobody handed to a
    // floor. A `let` is scoped to its function, so the answer is bounded by one.
    if at < the_function_holding(text, before_the_call) {
        return None;
    }
    // **Its own `;`, not the first one below it.** The next semicolon anywhere
    // would cut a binding whose value holds a block —
    // `map_or(0, |text| { …; … })`, which is how this file reads a file that
    // may not be there — off in the middle of the closure. A truncated
    // expression handed to a rule that reads shapes is a rule reading
    // something nobody wrote.
    let ends = the_end_of_the_statement_at(&text[at..]).map_or(text.len(), |end| at + end);
    Some(text[at..ends].trim().to_owned())
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Where the function holding `before` opens, so a name search cannot leave it.
///
/// Read as *the nearest line above that opens a function*, at whatever
/// indentation: this file writes them at column zero and inside two test
/// modules, and a rule that knew only one of those would bound half the file at
/// its top.
fn the_function_holding(text: &str, before: usize) -> usize {
    let mut end = before;
    while let Some(opened) = text[..end].rfind('\n') {
        let start = opened + 1;
        let stops = text[start..end].find('\n').map_or(end, |at| start + at);
        let line = text[start..stops].trim_start();
        if line.starts_with("fn ")
            || line.starts_with("pub fn ")
            || line.starts_with("pub(crate) fn ")
        {
            return start;
        }
        end = opened;
    }
    0
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Where the statement starting at `text` ends, counting brackets.
///
/// The caller hands this text whose comments and string contents are already
/// spent, so a `;` inside either is not one this can see; what is left is
/// nesting.
fn the_end_of_the_statement_at(text: &str) -> Option<usize> {
    let mut depth = 0_i32;
    for (at, character) in text.char_indices() {
        match character {
            '(' | '[' | '{' => depth += 1,
            ')' | ']' | '}' => depth -= 1,
            ';' if depth <= 0 => return Some(at),
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    None
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Where `name`'s nearest binding above `before` writes its expression.
///
/// **A rule rather than a list of spellings.** `let x = `, `let mut x = `,
/// `let x: usize = ` and `let mut x: Vec<u8> = ` are one binding written four
/// ways; a needle spelled `let {name} = ` reads the first and walks past the
/// rest. `check.rs` writes `let total: usize = counts.iter().sum();` under the
/// marker census, so with such a needle the floor rule cannot read the
/// expression of a floor in its own file, and one keyword (`mut`, or `: usize`)
/// switches that floor off with the rule green.
///
/// The binding that counts is the nearest one *above* the call, which is the
/// one the compiler resolves. An annotation is skipped to the `=` that opens
/// the value, bounded by the statement's own `;` so a `let x: usize;` with no
/// value does not reach into the next statement for one.
fn the_binding_of(name: &str, text: &str, before: usize) -> Option<usize> {
    let mut at = before;
    while let Some(opened) = text[..at].rfind("let ") {
        let after = &text[opened + "let ".len()..];
        let after = after.strip_prefix("mut ").unwrap_or(after);
        let bound: String = after
            .chars()
            .take_while(|character| character.is_alphanumeric() || *character == '_')
            .collect();
        if bound == name && !bound.is_empty() {
            let rest = after[bound.len()..].trim_start();
            let statement = rest.find(';').unwrap_or(rest.len());
            let rest = if let Some(annotated) = rest.strip_prefix(':') {
                let equals = annotated[..statement.saturating_sub(1)].find('=')?;
                &annotated[equals..]
            } else {
                rest
            };
            let value = rest.strip_prefix('=')?.trim_start();
            return Some(text.len() - value.len());
        }
        at = opened;
    }
    None
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Whether the byte at `at` sits inside one of this file's modules of tests.
///
/// A module of tests is `#[cfg(test)]` at column zero followed by `mod`, and
/// its body ends at the next column-zero `}` — every item inside it is
/// indented, so the first one that is not belongs to the module.
///
/// This is asked of a local binding and of the census walk itself. Of a name,
/// because `named`, `said` and `problem` are as ordinary in a fixture as in a
/// step, and following one inside a test module pulls thirty-four fixture
/// strings in as verdicts. Of the walk, which is the
/// stronger of the two — a fixture is a sentence written to be fed to a rule
/// rather than printed by one, and `strings` on the built binary finds none of
/// them, so a row for one is a row for something the gate cannot say.
fn inside_a_test_module(text: &str, at: usize) -> bool {
    in_a_test_region(&the_test_regions(text), at)
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Whether `at` falls in one of the spans [`the_test_regions`] found.
///
/// Held apart from the walk that finds them so a caller reading a whole file
/// computes the spans once.
fn in_a_test_region(regions: &[(usize, usize)], at: usize) -> bool {
    regions
        .iter()
        .any(|(opened, ended)| (*opened..*ended).contains(&at))
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Every file in this repository a marker could be written in, with its text.
///
/// A hatch is written in a comment, and this tree writes comments in Rust, SQL,
/// TOML, Cedar, YAML, Markdown and shell. Reading the text once per file keeps
/// the check above from opening the tree per excused row.
fn the_files_that_excuse_a_line(root: &Path) -> Vec<(String, String)> {
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    let _ = crate::gates::walk(root, &mut |path| {
        let readable = path.extension().is_some_and(|kind| {
            matches!(
                kind.to_string_lossy().as_ref(),
                "rs" | "sql" | "toml" | "cedar" | "cedarschema" | "yml" | "yaml" | "md" | "sh"
            )
        });
        if readable && let Ok(text) = std::fs::read_to_string(path) {
            found.push((crate::relative_to(root, path), text));
        }
    });
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Whether `text` uses `marker` to excuse a line, rather than merely holding it.
///
/// A hatch stands in a comment, after the opener its file uses — `--` in SQL,
/// `//` in Rust, `#` in YAML — and the markers are deliberately not spelled out
/// here, because a census counts one wherever it is written, and three named in
/// this paragraph would move three totals. A constant's own declaration is not one,
/// and neither
/// is prose that names a marker — which is why the opener has to be there and
/// why a `"` in front of it disqualifies the occurrence.
fn excuses_a_line_with(text: &str, marker: &str) -> bool {
    text.lines().any(|line| {
        line.find(marker).is_some_and(|at| {
            let before = line[..at].trim_end();
            (before.ends_with("//") || before.ends_with("--") || before.ends_with('#'))
                && !before.ends_with('"')
        })
    })
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Whether a constant's value is spelled the way this tree spells a marker.
///
/// **The spelling is said out loud because the walk reads the whole tree**, and
/// `"/-._~;=:"` — a URL's unreserved characters — ends with a colon and holds a
/// dash: a looser predicate reads it as a hatch.
///
/// **And it is no narrower than a working hatch.** Written as *lowercase
/// letters, digits, dashes and colons*, it cannot see the count marker
/// written with an underscore in place of its dash, or the same marker written
/// in capitals — two ordinary spellings of a working hatch, either of which
/// switches a gate off one line at a time with this census silent. A
/// marker begins with a letter and is made of letters, digits, dashes,
/// underscores and colons, ending in one.
///
/// It is a spelling and it knows nothing else, so it says yes to `postgres:`
/// too. That is what [`NOT_A_HATCH`] is for: the alternative is renaming
/// working code to satisfy a gate, or counting every `postgres:` in the tree as
/// an excused line.
fn spelled_like_a_marker(value: &str) -> bool {
    value.ends_with(':')
        && value.starts_with(|first: char| first.is_ascii_alphabetic())
        && value
            .chars()
            .all(|character| character.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || "-_:".contains(character))
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Constants spelled like a marker that are not one.
///
/// Empty, and that is the strongest state it can be in: nothing in this tree is
/// spelled like a hatch without being one. It exists because
/// [`spelled_like_a_marker`] is a spelling rule, and a spelling rule cannot know
/// what a constant is *for* — `postgres:`, `https:` and `warning:` are ordinary
/// values that would land in the census under a message about escape hatches,
/// and without this the only ways out are to rename working code or to put the
/// value in `MARKERS`, which makes the marker census count every occurrence of
/// it in the tree as an excused line.
///
/// A row here is checked against what the walk found, so it cannot outlive the
/// constant it excuses.
const NOT_A_HATCH: [&str; 0] = [];

#[cfg(test)]
/// Every span of this file that `#[cfg(test)]` keeps out of the binary.
///
/// **Read out of code, not out of text that still holds its literals.** A
/// walk from `"\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod "` to the next `"\n}\n"` over the raw
/// source lets a *string literal* containing either open a module that is not
/// there or close one early — and both directions are live. Measured with such
/// a walk: a fixture holding those bytes, planted in a step's own function, put
/// that whole function inside a phantom test module and made the census ask for
/// four real verdicts to be deleted from the record; and `the_item_reader_finds_the_item_it_is_asked_for`'s
/// Rust-source fixture, which is *already in this file*, has a `}` at column
/// zero that, read over the raw source, ends `mod the_meta_gate` early —
/// measured, eight rows of a record read that way were strings the built
/// binary does not contain. Reading
/// [`crate::gates::blanked`] spends every literal for a space and keeps every
/// offset, which is what a caller that indexes back into the source needs.
///
/// **And `#[cfg(test)]` on a function, not on a module alone.** Three of those
/// eight rows came from `#[cfg(test)] fn the_hatches_are_the_ones_written_down`
/// — a verdict producer for the *test*, compiled out like any other. The
/// attribute is what decides, so the item under it is read rather than assumed:
/// a `mod`, `fn`, `impl` or `pub` item ends at the next column-zero `}`, and a
/// `const`, `static` or `use` ends at its own `;`.
fn the_test_regions(text: &str) -> Vec<(usize, usize)> {
    /// The attributes that keep an item out of the binary.
    ///
    /// **`#[cfg(test)]` is not the only spelling.** `#[cfg(all(test, not(miri)))]`
    /// is ordinary Rust, and read as opening no region, widening a guard the way
    /// a maintainer reasonably would puts eleven of this file's own fixture
    /// strings into the record.
    ///
    /// **`#[cfg_attr(test, …)]` is *not* the same shape, and reading it as one
    /// is wrong in both directions at once.** It applies an attribute under a
    /// condition; it removes nothing from the binary — `gates.rs` reads it
    /// correctly one file over, as "a `cfg_attr`, which can add `#[ignore]`
    /// under a condition". Treated as a region opener, an ordinary
    /// `#[cfg_attr(test, allow(clippy::too_many_lines))]` on a shipped function
    /// deletes that function's whole body from the census: three verdicts still
    /// in the binary are reported as removed, and the edit that message invites
    /// is deleting their rows. And the mirror — a new `ui::warn("skipped: …")`
    /// added inside such a function is invisible from the start.
    const OPENS: [&str; 2] = ["#[cfg(test)]\n", "#[cfg(all(test,"];
    let code = crate::gates::blanked(text);
    // **`blanked` preserves characters, and this file is full of `§`, `—` and
    // `…`, so its *byte* offsets are not this text's.** Its own paragraph says
    // "whatever is here at character `n`" and the caller here indexes by byte.
    // Measured: regions computed in one and used in the other open two
    // thousand lines away from any attribute and take `check failed: {}` and
    // `not run: {skip}` — two things the gate really does say — out of the
    // record. A census that reports guards nobody removed teaches a reader to
    // move the number, which is how the removal completes.
    let starts: Vec<usize> = text.char_indices().map(|(at, _)| at).collect();
    assert_eq!(
        code.chars().count(),
        starts.len(),
        "the stripper is character-for-character with its input, and this map rests on it"
    );
    let at_byte = |in_code: usize| -> usize {
        let characters = code[..in_code.min(code.len())].chars().count();
        starts.get(characters).copied().unwrap_or(text.len())
    };
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    let mut from = 0_usize;
    while let Some((offset, opener)) = OPENS
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|opener| code[from..].find(opener).map(|at| (at, *opener)))
        .min_by_key(|(at, _)| *at)
    {
        let opened = from + offset;
        // **At a line start, and the file's first line is one.** Searched as
        // `"\n#[cfg(test)]\n"`, an attribute on line one is not an attribute —
        // and a `#[cfg(test)]` written after code on the same line is not one
        // either, so the newline still has to be there when there is a line
        // above.
        if opened != 0 && !code[..opened].ends_with('\n') {
            from = opened + opener.len();
            continue;
        }
        // The attribute may wrap or carry arguments, so the item begins after the
        // line the attribute ends on rather than a fixed number of bytes along.
        let mut item = code[opened..]
            .find('\n')
            .map_or(code.len(), |end| opened + end + 1);
        // Whatever stands between the attribute and the item it guards: a doc
        // block, or another attribute. Fourteen of this file's thirty-two are
        // written that way.
        while let Some(end) = code[item..].find('\n') {
            let line = code[item..item + end].trim_start();
            if line.starts_with("//") || line.starts_with("#[") || line.is_empty() {
                item += end + 1;
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }
        // **The item may wear a visibility, and it may be a `type`.** A
        // classifier that reads the keyword sitting at `item` and nothing else
        // reads `pub(crate) const`, `pub static`, `pub use` and a `type … = …;`
        // under a `#[cfg(test)]` each as a *brace* item, and the region then
        // runs to the next column-zero `}` — deleting from **both** censuses
        // whatever shipped code stands in between, and with it every verdict
        // that could still be reworded. `xtask/src` holds such items —
        // `dev.rs` and `local.rs` each declare a test-only `pub(crate) const`
        // — and the shape costs one keyword to introduce and turns the record
        // off without a word.
        let head = {
            let rest = code[item..].trim_start();
            match rest.strip_prefix("pub") {
                Some(after) => match after.chars().next() {
                    Some(' ' | '\n') => after.trim_start(),
                    // `pub(crate)`, `pub(super)`, `pub(in a::path)`.
                    Some('(') => after[1..]
                        .find(')')
                        .map_or(rest, |end| after[end + 2..].trim_start()),
                    _ => rest,
                },
                None => rest,
            }
        };
        let statement = ["const ", "static ", "use ", "type ", "extern crate "]
            .iter()
            .any(|opener| head.starts_with(opener))
            // **`mod` is written both ways**, and only what follows the name
            // tells them apart. `mod tests { … }` is the common one and a `;`
            // inside its body must not end the region; `mod name;` ends at the
            // first one. Deciding it by the keyword alone would break whichever
            // of the two got the other's rule.
            || (head.starts_with("mod ")
                && match (head.find(';'), head.find('{')) {
                    (Some(semicolon), Some(brace)) => semicolon < brace,
                    (Some(_), None) => true,
                    _ => false,
                });
        let ended = if statement {
            // **The `;` that ends the statement, not one inside its type.**
            // `[&str; 4]` holds a semicolon, and every written-down list in this
            // file is spelled that way — `WHAT_CI_RUNS`, `EXCUSED`, `EXCUSED_IN`,
            // `WHAT_THE_GATE_CAN_SAY`, `NOT_A_HATCH`. Ending the region inside the
            // type would read the *value* of each as shipped code, and a fixture
            // array holding a call spelling would put its own strings into the
            // record. Depth counts brackets and braces, which is what tells the
            // two semicolons apart.
            // **`<` and `>` are not brackets.** Counted as a pair, the `->` of a
            // `fn(&str) -> bool` in a declared type cancels the `[` that opens
            // the array, so the region ends at the `;` *inside* the type and the
            // const's value reads as shipped code — which is exactly the
            // failure this counter exists to prevent. A lone `<` fails the
            // other way, running the region past its `;` and swallowing real
            // verdicts. Only the three bracket pairs nest here; a generic
            // argument that needs to hold a `;` holds it inside one of them.
            let mut depth = 0_isize;
            code[item..]
                .char_indices()
                .find(|(_, character)| {
                    match character {
                        '[' | '(' | '{' => depth += 1,
                        ']' | ')' | '}' => depth -= 1,
                        _ => {}
                    }
                    *character == ';' && depth == 0
                })
                .map_or(code.len(), |(end, _)| item + end + 1)
        } else {
            code[item..]
                .find("\n}\n")
                .map_or(code.len(), |end| item + end + 3)
        };
        found.push((at_byte(opened), at_byte(ended)));
        from = item.max(opened + opener.len());
    }
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The sentence a name holds, when a verdict travels by one.
///
/// A verdict is not always a literal at the call. `const NO_CATALOG: &str = "…";`
/// handed to `ui::failure(NO_CATALOG)`, and `let said = format!("…"); moved
/// .push(said);` — which this file already writes twice — both carry no quote
/// where the scan looks, so a reader that only looks there walks past them. This
/// resolves the name where it is bound, whether by `const` or by `let`, and
/// through a `format!` or a `concat!` if one stands between.
///
/// The first binding in the file wins. Shadowing would make that the wrong one,
/// and reading the wrong sentence errs toward *more* being written down, which
/// is the direction this census is safe in: a sentence the gate cannot say is a
/// line a reviewer deletes, and a sentence it can say that nobody wrote is the
/// defect.
fn the_sentence_bound_to(
    after: &str,
    text: &str,
    before_the_call: usize,
    regions: &[(usize, usize)],
) -> Option<String> {
    let name: String = after
        .trim_start()
        .chars()
        .take_while(|character| character.is_alphanumeric() || *character == '_')
        .collect();
    if name.len() < 2 {
        return None;
    }
    // **A pattern is not a call.** `Err(output) => { … }` matches the `Err(`
    // needle and binds a name, and with a local binding resolvable the reader
    // would follow that name to whatever `let output = …` stands above the
    // arm — in this file, a test's fixture of cargo's output, reported twice as
    // a verdict the gate can say. What tells them apart is the `=>` that closes
    // the pattern: a call's argument is followed by a bracket the expression
    // goes on from, a pattern's by the arm.
    if after.trim_start()[name.len()..]
        .trim_start()
        .starts_with(')')
        && after.trim_start()[name.len()..]
            .trim_start()
            .trim_start_matches(')')
            .trim_start()
            .starts_with("=>")
    {
        return None;
    }
    // **`const` and `let`, which is what the paragraph above promises.** Read
    // for `const` alone, every verdict travelling by a local binding is
    // outside the census — including all three sentences of
    // `the_hatches_are_the_ones_written_down`, the per-marker escape-hatch
    // census, whose whole job is to make "a gate switched off for one line"
    // visible. Measured: its own guard loop — the one whose comment opens
    // "The other direction" — is then deletable with every xtask test green,
    // because its sentence travels by a `let` nothing follows.
    //
    // **A `const` is looked up in the file and a `let` above the call.** A
    // `const` verdict is written in SCREAMING_CASE and once; a `let` is a
    // local, and names like `said`, `problem` and `named` are written in
    // dozens of functions. Taking the file's first `let named =` pulls fixture
    // strings in as verdicts — measured, thirty-one of them, most
    // being format fragments from this file's own tests. So the binding that
    // counts is the nearest one *above* the call, which is the one the
    // compiler would resolve.
    //
    // **And a `let` only where the gate runs.** A module of tests binds
    // `named`, `said` and `problem` as freely as a step does, and resolving
    // those pulls thirty-four fixture strings into the census as verdicts —
    // a list that says the gate can say things it cannot is as useless as one
    // that misses what it can. What the gate says is what it says outside its
    // own tests.
    let local = !in_a_test_region(regions, before_the_call);
    // A `const` is written once and in SCREAMING_CASE, so it is looked up in
    // the file; a `let` is a local and is read by rule, from the nearest
    // binding above the call — see [`the_binding_of`] for why a list of
    // spellings here would be three of a larger number.
    let declared = format!("const {name}: &str =");
    let places = [
        text.find(&declared).map(|found| found + declared.len()),
        local
            .then(|| the_binding_of(&name, text, before_the_call))
            .flatten(),
    ];
    for at in places.into_iter().flatten() {
        let Some(offset) = text[at..].find('"') else {
            continue;
        };
        let quote = at + offset;
        // What may stand between the binding and its literal is a `format!` or
        // a `concat!` and nothing else — otherwise the quote found belongs to
        // some later statement.
        let between: String = text[at..quote]
            .chars()
            .filter(|character| !character.is_whitespace())
            .collect();
        if !matches!(between.as_str(), "" | "format!(" | "concat!(") {
            continue;
        }
        return Some(if between == "concat!(" {
            the_concatenation_at(&text[quote..], None).0
        } else {
            the_literal_at(&text[quote..]).0
        });
    }
    None
}
#[cfg(test)]
/// Every literal of a `concat!`, joined, and how many bytes the call took.
///
/// `concat!` is how a verdict too long for one literal is written without a
/// wrap, and its pieces are one sentence. Reading only the first piece would
/// pin half a sentence and leave the other half free.
fn the_concatenation_at(text: &str, first: Option<usize>) -> (String, usize) {
    let mut sentence = String::new();
    let mut at = 0_usize;
    while let Some(offset) = text[at..].find('"') {
        let quote = at + offset;
        // The call ends at its closing bracket, and a quote past it belongs to
        // the next statement.
        if text[at..quote].contains(')') {
            break;
        }
        // **Each piece read the way it is spelled.** A `concat!` may hold a raw
        // string anywhere in it — that is half of why it is written — and
        // reading one with the plain reader returns the fragment before its
        // first inner quote.
        // **The first piece's prefix is behind the caller's slice.** This text
        // begins *at* the opening quote, so `text[0..0]` is empty and the `r#`
        // that told the caller this was a raw string is invisible here. With only
        // the caller's test widened, `concat!(r#"… "nothing" …"#, " …")` is
        // recorded with its inner quotes and everything between them gone, and
        // the unpinned half can then be reworded with the census green. So the
        // caller hands its answer down.
        let hashes = if at == 0 {
            first
        } else {
            the_raw_opener_in(
                &text[at..quote]
                    .chars()
                    .filter(|character| !character.is_whitespace())
                    .collect::<String>(),
            )
            .map(|(_, hashes)| hashes)
        };
        let (piece, length) = match hashes {
            Some(hashes) => the_raw_literal_at(&text[quote..], hashes),
            None => the_literal_at(&text[quote..]),
        };
        sentence.push_str(&piece);
        at = quote + length;
    }
    (sentence, at)
}
#[cfg(test)]
/// Every top-level part of an argument or parameter list.
///
/// Split at the commas no bracket is open over, so a `Vec<A, B>` or a nested
/// call stays one part.
fn the_parts_of(list: &str) -> Vec<&str> {
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    let mut depth = 0_isize;
    let mut from = 0_usize;
    for (at, character) in list.char_indices() {
        match character {
            '(' | '[' | '{' | '<' => depth += 1,
            ')' | ']' | '}' | '>' => depth -= 1,
            ',' if depth == 0 => {
                found.push(&list[from..at]);
                from = at + 1;
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    found.push(&list[from..]);
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The sentences a *parameter* can hold, read from the calls that fill it.
///
/// **The fifth way a verdict travels.** `the_sentences_a_name_can_hold` follows
/// an interpolated name to a `let` in the same function; a name that is a
/// *parameter* has no `let`, and the clause it carries is written at each call
/// site instead. `gates.rs`'s `hidden(file, at, "a condition on the whole file")`
/// is that shape: without this, the template
/// `this test is hidden from the run by {by}` is in the record and none of the
/// four clauses that fill `{by}` is, so the informative half of a real
/// diagnostic can be rewritten to anything with the census silent.
///
/// Measured against the built binary, which is the direction a census of
/// removals never asks: `strings target/debug/xtask` holds
/// `a condition on the whole file`, and a record without this does not.
fn the_sentences_a_parameter_can_hold(
    name: &str,
    text: &str,
    before_the_call: usize,
    regions: &[(usize, usize)],
) -> Vec<String> {
    let Some(header) = text[..before_the_call].rfind("\nfn ") else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    let opened = header + "\nfn ".len();
    let called: String = text[opened..]
        .chars()
        .take_while(|character| character.is_alphanumeric() || *character == '_')
        .collect();
    if called.len() < 2 {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    let Some(list) = text[opened..].find('(').map(|at| opened + at) else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    // **After the bracket, which is what every other caller passes.** Handed the
    // slice starting *at* the `(`, the depth reaches one on the bracket itself
    // and the scan returns the *second* unmatched `)` — or, where the code after
    // a call is paren-balanced to the end of the file, `None`, and the call site
    // is dropped. Measured that way: three of the six live call sites of one
    // helper invisible, and 429 of 1193 call sites of column-zero functions
    // across `xtask/src`. The three clauses that fall out —
    // `the blanket-allow walk`, `the emitter's determinism walk` and the
    // `§15.1` one — are all in the built binary.
    let Some(closes) = the_matching_bracket_of(&text[list + 1..]).map(|end| list + 1 + end) else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    let Some(index) = the_parts_of(&text[list + 1..closes])
        .iter()
        .position(|part| {
            part.split(':')
                .next()
                .unwrap_or_default()
                .trim()
                .trim_start_matches("mut ")
                .trim()
                == name
        })
    else {
        return Vec::new();
    };
    let needle = format!("{called}(");
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    let mut from = 0_usize;
    while let Some(offset) = text[from..].find(&needle) {
        let at = from + offset;
        from = at + needle.len();
        // The definition is not a call site, a mention of the name inside a
        // longer one is not one, and a call in a module of tests says nothing
        // the gate can say — measured, two `the probe walk` rows the built
        // binary does not contain.
        if in_a_test_region(regions, at)
            || text[..at].ends_with("fn ")
            || text[..at]
                .chars()
                .next_back()
                .is_some_and(|before| before.is_alphanumeric() || before == '_')
        {
            continue;
        }
        let Some(end) = the_matching_bracket_of(&text[from..]).map(|end| from + end) else {
            continue;
        };
        let Some(argument) = the_parts_of(&text[from..end])
            .get(index)
            .map(|part| part.trim())
        else {
            continue;
        };
        if argument.starts_with('"') {
            found.push(the_literal_at(argument).0);
        } else if argument
            .chars()
            .all(|character| character.is_alphanumeric() || character == '_')
            && argument.len() >= 2
        {
            // **And a name, which is how three of the four clauses of this
            // function's own worked example are written.** The doc above cites
            // `hidden(file, at, "a condition on the whole file")` and says the
            // gap it closes is *all four* clauses of `{by}`; a literal at the call
            // reaches only the one spelled that way. The other
            // three come from `findings.push(hidden(file, *at, what))`, where
            // `what` is a local choice — and `strings` on the built binary holds
            // every one of them.
            found.extend(the_sentences_a_binding_can_hold(argument, text, at));
        }
    }
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The sentences a verdict's interpolated clause can be.
///
/// **A template is not a sentence.** The census reads the outer literal of a
/// message and never what is put into it, and this tree writes the informative
/// half of a line as a clause chosen just above the call:
///
/// ```text
/// let known = if declared.contains(&code) { "…as text" } else { "…and it is in no registry" };
/// findings.push(Finding { what: format!("`{code}` is {known}"), … });
/// ```
///
/// Without this, the template is pinned and both clauses are free, so the
/// distinction the diagnostic exists to draw can be deleted with no number
/// moving anywhere. Measured: eight such clauses outside the list, across five
/// files, including the
/// reason four steps give for skipping in a bare checkout — whose own doc says
/// "a step that reports the second as the first costs the reader the whole
/// diagnosis".
///
/// Every literal of the binding's own statement is read, not only the ones in
/// answer position: a name that is not a sentence resolves to nothing, and
/// reading one literal too many errs toward *more* being written down, which
/// is the direction this census is safe in.
fn the_sentences_a_name_can_hold(
    name: &str,
    text: &str,
    before_the_call: usize,
    regions: &[(usize, usize)],
) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut found = the_sentences_a_parameter_can_hold(name, text, before_the_call, regions);
    found.extend(the_sentences_a_binding_can_hold(
        name,
        text,
        before_the_call,
    ));
    found.sort();
    found.dedup();
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The sentences a local `let` binding can hold.
///
/// Held apart from [`the_sentences_a_name_can_hold`] so the parameter reader can
/// ask it without asking itself: a call-site argument that is a *name* rather
/// than a literal is resolved here, and resolving it through the whole of the
/// name reader would recur.
fn the_sentences_a_binding_can_hold(name: &str, text: &str, before_the_call: usize) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    for declared in [format!("let {name} = "), format!("let Some({name}) = ")] {
        let Some(at) = text[..before_the_call].rfind(&declared) else {
            continue;
        };
        let from = at + declared.len();
        let rest = &text[from..];
        // **Only a choice between written clauses.** Anything else — a call, a
        // count, a path — is not a sentence, and scanning an arbitrary
        // right-hand side for quotes reads a `"` that opens nothing and walks
        // off the end of the file.
        if !(rest.starts_with("if ") || rest.starts_with("match ")) {
            break;
        }
        // The choice ends at the first `;` no bracket is open over. A `;`
        // inside a literal is text, so the quote state decides.
        let mut depth = 0_i32;
        let mut quoted = false;
        let mut escaped = false;
        let mut ends = rest.len();
        for (offset, character) in rest.char_indices() {
            match character {
                _ if escaped => escaped = false,
                '\\' if quoted => escaped = true,
                '"' => quoted = !quoted,
                _ if quoted => {}
                '{' | '(' | '[' => depth += 1,
                '}' | ')' | ']' => depth -= 1,
                ';' if depth <= 0 => {
                    ends = offset;
                    break;
                }
                _ => {}
            }
        }
        found.extend(the_clauses_of(&rest[..ends]));
        break;
    }
    found.sort();
    found.dedup();
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Every literal an `if` or a `match` can answer with.
///
/// The same rule the reader of a function's answers uses: a literal in answer
/// position, with a `format!` or a `concat!` allowed to stand between the
/// answer and its words. Named because a choice reaches a verdict three ways —
/// bound to a name above the call, returned by a helper, and written *at* the
/// call — and all three read it alike.
fn the_clauses_of(body: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut found: Vec<(usize, String)> = Vec::new();
    for anchor in ["=>", "{", "else"] {
        let mut walked = 0_usize;
        while let Some(offset) = body[walked..].find(anchor) {
            let after = walked + offset + anchor.len();
            walked = after;
            let Some(quote) = body[after..].find('"').map(|offset| after + offset) else {
                break;
            };
            let between: String = body[after..quote]
                .chars()
                .filter(|character| !character.is_whitespace())
                .collect();
            // **And `Some(`, which is how an arm answers "there is a sentence".**
            // `Ok(_) => None, Err(_) => Some("…")` is an ordinary shape and the
            // literal is still that clause's own words.
            // **A block's tail expression is not read, and that is a written
            // gap rather than an oversight.** `if … { if … { continue; }
            // "a condition" }` answers with its last expression, and `between`
            // is then the statements that came first. One clause of the very
            // verdict the parameter reader's doc cites — `gates.rs`'s
            // `"a condition"`, which `strings` finds in the binary — is
            // unreachable this way.
            //
            // The obvious rule does not close it. "A tail follows a `;` or a
            // `}`" matches far more than a tail: the `"` found after such a
            // `between` is often inside a literal already consumed, and
            // `the_literal_at` then walks off the end of the file — measured on
            // this tree, with brackets balanced and again with quotes balanced
            // as well. A rule that needs a third guess is a rule that is not
            // written yet, and a scanner that panics is worse than one that
            // misses a row.
            if !matches!(
                between.as_str(),
                "" | "format!(" | "concat!(" | "{" | "Some(" | "{Some("
            ) {
                continue;
            }
            let (sentence, _) = if between == "concat!(" {
                the_concatenation_at(&body[quote..], None)
            } else {
                the_literal_at(&body[quote..])
            };
            // **A clause with no words is a value.** `{schema}.{name}` is a
            // table name the limits gate pushes and `{written} = {subject}` is
            // a floor's own expression; neither is something the gate *says*,
            // and putting them in the list would make it a bag of fragments
            // rather than a record of verdicts. A verdict has words.
            let has_words = sentence
                .split(['{', '}'])
                .step_by(2)
                .any(|outside| outside.chars().any(char::is_alphabetic));
            if !sentence.is_empty() && has_words {
                found.push((quote, sentence));
            }
        }
    }
    // **One literal is one clause, however many anchors reach it.** `else {`
    // matches both the `else` anchor and the `{` anchor, and `=> {` matches both
    // `=>` and `{`, so read per anchor every braced clause is read twice and
    // the list holds a phantom second copy of it. That is not a cosmetic
    // duplicate: the census
    // reports a missing row as *"the gate no longer says X, so a guard that can
    // reach that verdict was removed"*, and with a doubled row that sentence
    // fires when a branch is merely **inverted** — measured on
    // `if found { "the door's" } else { "unrelated" }` rewritten as
    // `if !found { "unrelated" } else { "the door's" }`, same two words, same
    // two cases, no guard touched. A maintainer who believes the message
    // deletes a row that is still reachable.
    //
    // Deduplicated by *position* and never by sentence: two guards that happen
    // to say the same words are two guards, and the list is a multiset on
    // purpose.
    found.sort_by_key(|(quote, _)| *quote);
    found.dedup_by_key(|(quote, _)| *quote);
    found.into_iter().map(|(_, sentence)| sentence).collect()
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Where the call that opens at `at` closes, so a choice inside it is bounded.
///
/// A literal past the call's own bracket belongs to the next statement, which
/// is the mistake the rule above this one exists to stop.
fn the_end_of_the_call(text: &str, at: usize) -> usize {
    the_matching_bracket_of(&text[at..]).map_or(text.len(), |end| at + end)
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The names a message puts something into, in the order it writes them.
///
/// `{code}` and `{count:?}` both name `code` and `count`; `{}` and `{{` name
/// nothing. Only a name can be followed to a binding.
fn the_names_interpolated_into(sentence: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut names = Vec::new();
    let characters: Vec<char> = sentence.chars().collect();
    let mut at = 0_usize;
    while at < characters.len() {
        if characters[at] != '{' {
            at += 1;
            continue;
        }
        if characters.get(at + 1) == Some(&'{') {
            at += 2;
            continue;
        }
        let name: String = characters[at + 1..]
            .iter()
            .take_while(|character| character.is_alphanumeric() || **character == '_')
            .collect();
        at += 1 + name.chars().count().max(1);
        if name.len() >= 2 && !name.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_numeric) {
            names.push(name);
        }
    }
    names
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Where the bracket opened before `text` closes, counting the ones between.
///
/// An argument may be a call of its own, and the first `)` then closes that
/// argument rather than the call the scanner is reading.
fn the_matching_bracket_of(text: &str) -> Option<usize> {
    let mut depth = 0_usize;
    let mut quoted = false;
    let mut escaped = false;
    let mut skip_to = 0_usize;
    for (at, character) in text.char_indices() {
        if at < skip_to {
            continue;
        }
        // **And a char literal is not a quote.** A double quote written inside a
        // char literal, read as an opening quote, hides every bracket after it:
        // the call then has no end and its floor's sentence leaves the record —
        // the same wrong direction the paragraph below is about.
        if !quoted
            && character == '\''
            && let Some(ends) = a_char_literal_at(&text[at..])
        {
            skip_to = at + ends;
            continue;
        }
        // **A bracket inside a verdict's own words is text.** Blind to quotes,
        // a counter ends a call at the first `)` a sentence happens to contain
        // — and the clauses after it stop being read, so the census reports a
        // *still-shipped* verdict as one the gate "no longer says". That
        // message tells a maintainer a guard was removed, and the edit it
        // invites is deleting the row, which unpins the verdict. An unmatched
        // `(` fails loudly instead; only `)` is quiet.
        match character {
            _ if escaped => escaped = false,
            '\\' if quoted => escaped = true,
            '"' => quoted = !quoted,
            _ if quoted => {}
            '(' => depth += 1,
            ')' if depth == 0 => return Some(at),
            ')' => depth -= 1,
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    None
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// How long the char literal at the start of `text` is, if there is one.
///
/// A quote mark in Rust opens a lifetime far more often than a literal, so what
/// decides it is the *closing* mark rather than the opening one: a one-character
/// literal and an escape both close within a few characters, and a lifetime name
/// never does. Returns the length including both marks.
fn a_char_literal_at(text: &str) -> Option<usize> {
    let rest = text.strip_prefix('\'')?;
    let mut characters = rest.char_indices();
    let (_, first) = characters.next()?;
    if first == '\\' {
        // An escape runs to the `'` that closes it, which is at most a few
        // characters away — `\u{10FFFF}` is the longest Rust writes.
        let ends = rest.get(1..)?.find('\'')?;
        return (ends <= 10).then_some(1 + 1 + ends + 1);
    }
    let (after, _) = characters.next().unwrap_or((rest.len(), ' '));
    (rest[after..].starts_with('\'')).then_some(1 + after + 1)
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// The arguments of a call, split at a comma no bracket and no quote holds.
///
/// `rsplit(',')` is wrong here in two ways at once: a comma inside a literal
/// argument cuts the literal in half, and a call rustfmt has wrapped ends with a
/// trailing comma, so the last piece is the empty string and the call is skipped
/// — measured, `exactly(` in the list of floor helpers matching none of its
/// three call sites.
fn the_arguments_in(arguments: &str) -> Vec<&str> {
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    let mut depth = 0_isize;
    let mut quoted = false;
    let mut escaped = false;
    let mut from = 0_usize;
    let mut skip_to = 0_usize;
    for (at, character) in arguments.char_indices() {
        if at < skip_to {
            continue;
        }
        // **A char literal is not a quote.** A double quote written *inside* a
        // char literal, which `text.matches(…).count()` does, read as an
        // opening quote swallows the rest of the call — the argument list
        // comes back as one piece and the floor's sentence leaves the record.
        if !quoted
            && character == '\''
            && let Some(ends) = a_char_literal_at(&arguments[at..])
        {
            skip_to = at + ends;
            continue;
        }
        match character {
            _ if escaped => escaped = false,
            '\\' if quoted => escaped = true,
            '"' => quoted = !quoted,
            _ if quoted => {}
            '(' | '[' | '{' => depth += 1,
            ')' | ']' | '}' => depth -= 1,
            ',' if depth == 0 => {
                found.push(arguments[from..at].trim());
                from = at + 1;
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    found.push(arguments[from..].trim());
    found.retain(|argument| !argument.is_empty());
    found
}

#[cfg(test)]
/// Every sentence handed to the floor helper, resolved from the name it
/// travels under.
///
/// `inspected_something(count, DRIFT)` turns a zero into a verdict, and the
/// verdict is the second argument: a `const` in this file, named at the call and
/// never written there. So the name is taken from the call and looked up where
/// it is bound, which is the same resolution a verdict passed by name gets
/// anywhere else.
fn the_floors_named_in(text: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    /// The three helpers a floor's subject can reach.
    const FLOORS: [&str; 3] = ["inspected_something(", "inspected_all(", "exactly("];

    let regions = the_test_regions(text);
    // **The code, not the text that reads like it.** This file writes a floor's
    // call out inside a string literal — the console record's rows carry the
    // condition that chooses each verdict, and a dozen of those read
    // `if let Err(problem) = inspected_something(read, "images")`. Over the
    // raw text the scan finds the mention, the bracket counter runs past its
    // escaped quotes, and the *next row of the table* is reported as a
    // sentence this gate can say. The rule that reads the same three helpers
    // one screen down reads code for the same reason.
    let code = the_code_in(text);
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    // **A floor, whichever helper it reaches.** There are three:
    // `inspected_something`, `inspected_all` and `exactly`. Named by the first
    // alone, the five subjects that travel by the other two — `COLOURS`,
    // `MIGRATIONS`, `MEMBERS`, `DOCUMENTS`, `WALKED` — are outside the record,
    // so each can be rewritten to congratulate the reader. `DOCUMENTS` is the
    // sentence printed when the document walk read nothing.
    //
    // Naming a function instead of naming *a floor* is this rule's own
    // commonest defect: a spelling closed and the class left.
    let mut at = 0_usize;
    while let Some((offset, helper)) = FLOORS
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|helper| code[at..].find(helper).map(|found| (found, *helper)))
        .min_by_key(|(found, _)| *found)
    {
        let from = at + offset + helper.len();
        at = from;
        // **The call's own closing bracket, counted rather than found.** The
        // *first* `)` makes `inspected_something(compiled.files.len(), DRIFT)`,
        // which is how the shipped call at the top of this file is written,
        // yield `compiled.files.len(`, which is skipped for not being an
        // identifier. `DRIFT` would then survive in the census only because this
        // file's unit tests name it with a plain argument; a floor written the
        // ordinary way would contribute nothing, and its sentence would be free.
        let Some(end) = the_matching_bracket_of(&text[from..]) else {
            continue;
        };
        // The last argument, and the closing bracket of the call rather than of
        // an argument: `compiled.files.len(), DRIFT)` closes twice.
        let arguments = &text[from..from + end];
        let Some(last) = the_arguments_in(arguments).pop() else {
            continue;
        };
        // **And a subject written where it is used.** A floor's sentence is
        // usually a `const` named at the call, but it may be the literal itself
        // — `exactly(declared, written, "declared setting")` writes it there —
        // and then there is nothing to resolve and no name to look up. Read as a
        // name, it fails the identifier test and the sentence stays outside
        // the record, free to be reworded into its own inverse.
        if let Some(written) = last
            .strip_prefix('"')
            .and_then(|rest| rest.strip_suffix('"'))
            .filter(|inner| !inner.contains('"'))
        {
            found.push(written.to_owned());
            continue;
        }
        if !last.chars().all(|c| c.is_alphanumeric() || c == '_') {
            continue;
        }
        if let Some(sentence) = the_sentence_bound_to(last, text, from, &regions) {
            found.push(sentence);
        }
    }
    // **Once per sentence, not once per call.** A floor is a `const` written in
    // one place and handed to the helper wherever a count is taken — including
    // from this file's own unit tests, which pass `DRIFT` to check the helper
    // rather than to say anything. The census compares multisets, so counting
    // the mentions would ask the list to hold a sentence as many times as
    // somebody happened to name it.
    found.sort();
    found.dedup();
    found
}

/// A call's line with the module path the callee is written under taken off.
///
/// The needles this census scans for are call spellings — `ui::warn(`,
/// `ui::failure(` — and a caller may write the same call under any path that
/// reaches it. `crate::` in front is not something standing *before* the call;
/// it is part of the call's own name, and reading it as the former makes the
/// position rule reject the site and skip its sentence.
///
/// Only whole `identifier::` groups come off, so a line that genuinely puts
/// something before the call — prose in a literal, an argument, an operator —
/// still fails the position test it is meant to fail.
#[cfg(test)]
fn without_a_path_qualifier(before: &str) -> &str {
    let mut rest = before;
    while let Some(head) = rest.strip_suffix("::") {
        let shorter = head
            .trim_end_matches(|character: char| character.is_alphanumeric() || character == '_');
        if shorter.len() == head.len() {
            break;
        }
        rest = shorter;
    }
    // **And the space the qualifier left behind**, which is what the sibling
    // rule for `.push(`'s receiver trims two screens up. Stripping
    // `crate::` off `Ok(_) => crate::` leaves `Ok(_) => ` — a trailing space —
    // so untrimmed, `ends_with("=>")` fails and the site is skipped: measured,
    // one match arm returning a path-qualified skip turned a step off with
    // `ok 1133 test(s)`, `check passed` and exit 0, while the same line without
    // `crate::` was caught. `{ ` is the same shape.
    rest.trim_end()
}

/// Every string literal that opens a `needle` call, `after` the named field.
///
/// **The two populations are one question.** `failures.push` names a verdict and
/// `findings.push(Finding { what: … })` writes a finding's sentence, and a guard
/// that can only be reached through the second is as deletable as one reached
/// through the first.
#[cfg(test)]
fn the_sentences_after(text: &str, needle: &str, field: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    // Once per file. Read per needle occurrence it costs a whole-file strip and
    // a character count per region each time — measured, 25 seconds for this
    // test where once per file takes 1.3.
    let regions = the_test_regions(text);
    let mut found = Vec::new();
    let mut at = 0_usize;
    while let Some(offset) = text[at..].find(needle) {
        let at_the_call = at + offset;
        // **A call, and not this function's own mention of one.** The needle
        // matches the place below where it is written *inside a string
        // literal*, so a reader taking every match finds itself and reports two
        // fragments of its own source as verdicts nobody had written down — the
        // same shape as a test that greps its own file. A real push begins a
        // statement, so what settles it is that nothing but indentation stands
        // between it and the start of its line — or a match arm's `=>`, which
        // is where eight of the seventy findings are pushed from and where a
        // rule of "indentation only" would read none of them.
        let line_began = text[..at_the_call].rfind('\n').map_or(0, |end| end + 1);
        let before = text[line_began..at_the_call].trim();
        at = at_the_call + needle.len();
        // **A test module says nothing the gate can say.** A fixture is a
        // sentence written to be *fed* to a rule rather than printed by one, and
        // it is compiled out: `strings` on the built `xtask` finds none of them.
        // Measured without it, thirty-three of six hundred rows were fixtures — `the door's`,
        // `harmless`, `a count that can rot`, `skipped: a probe, announced the
        // way a step announces one` — so a twentieth of the record was strings
        // the binary does not contain, in the list whose one job is to say what
        // it can print. A record that mixes the two cannot be read as either.
        if in_a_test_region(&regions, at_the_call) {
            continue;
        }
        if field.is_empty() {
            // A verdict begins a statement, or a match arm's `=>` — and for
            // `.push(` the receiver's own name stands in front of it, which is
            // an identifier and nothing else. `out.push(character)` is filtered
            // by the literal rule below, not here.
            let before = if matches!(needle, ".push(" | ".extend(") {
                // **And the space the receiver left behind.** Trimming the
                // identifier off `Ok(_) => drifted.push(` leaves `Ok(_) => `,
                // which is neither empty nor ending in `=>`, so untrimmed every
                // match-arm push is dropped — nine sites in `xtask/src`.
                // Measured that way, the drift arm's message rewritten to one
                // nobody wrote down:
                // `everything_the_gate_can_say_is_written_down ... ok`.
                // **The whole receiver path, not its last segment.** Trimming
                // identifier characters leaves `report.` in front of
                // `report.problems.push(…)`, and a dot is neither empty nor an
                // arrow nor a brace — so a verdict pushed onto a struct field
                // would be skipped entirely. Such sites are in `limits.rs`,
                // carrying values, and a sentence written the same way would be
                // free. Trimming the dots as well leaves nothing, which is what
                // a receiver should leave — and it still rejects this file's own
                // mentions of a call inside a literal, because those end in
                // prose rather than in a receiver.
                before
                    .trim_end_matches(|character: char| {
                        character.is_alphanumeric() || character == '_' || character == '.'
                    })
                    .trim_end()
            } else {
                before
            };
            // **And `return`, which is a keyword and not a receiver.** A needle
            // of `return Err(` is narrower than the language: a tail-position
            // `Err(format!(…))` is the same guard travelling the same way, and
            // seven of them — the database-unreachable message, both log-store
            // health messages, `cargo update refused`, `check failed` and the
            // two rustfmt ones — would be outside the census entirely. With the
            // needle `Err(`, a returned verdict has `return ` in front of it, so
            // the position rule has to know the keyword: a tail-position
            // `Err(format!(…))` is the same guard as a returned one, and the two
            // spellings must read alike.
            // **Until neither moves.** Stripped once each, keyword first and path
            // second, the two cannot compose: `return crate::ui::warn("…")`
            // leaves `return crate::`, the path strip takes it to `return`, and a
            // bare `return` is neither empty nor an arrow nor a brace — the site
            // is rejected. That is wrong in both directions at once. A shipped
            // skip rewritten to that spelling makes the census report *a guard
            // that can reach that verdict was removed* when nothing was removed,
            // and the edit that message invites is deleting the row; and a new
            // step-off written that way is invisible from the start.
            let mut before = before;
            loop {
                let shorter = without_a_path_qualifier(
                    before.strip_suffix("return").unwrap_or(before).trim_end(),
                );
                if shorter.len() == before.len() {
                    break;
                }
                before = shorter;
            }
            // **And the path the callee is written under, which is part of the
            // callee.** The needles name `ui::warn(`, and `crate::ui::warn(` is
            // the same call, and untrimmed it leaves `crate::` standing in front,
            // which is neither empty nor `=>` nor `{`, so the sentence is never
            // read. Measured, two lines at the top of a step —
            // `crate::ui::warn("skipped: …"); return;` — turned that step off
            // with `everything_the_gate_can_say_is_written_down ... ok`,
            // `check passed` and exit 0, and CI judges this gate by its exit
            // code. `self::`, `super::` and any module path read alike.

            // **And a block that opens on the same line.** A rule that a verdict
            // begins its line or follows a `=>` misses
            // `if !x { failures.push("…"); }`, which is neither — measured, a
            // guard written that way pushes a sentence in no list with the
            // census green. What the
            // rule is really for is telling a call from this file's own mentions
            // of one inside a literal, and a mention is preceded by prose rather
            // than by a brace.
            // **And a `let` binding.**
            // `let () = crate::ui::warn("skipped: …"); return;` leaves `let () =`
            // in front of the call — not empty, not
            // `=>`, not a brace — so under a list of names it turns the whole
            // dependency step off (licences, bans and sources over every crate)
            // with `check passed`, exit 0 and the census silent; and `()` is the
            // spelling clippy's own `ignored_unit_patterns` asks for, so nothing
            // else in the tree objects either.
            //
            // Written as *where code can open a call* rather than as a list of
            // names. What this rule is really for is telling a call from this
            // file's own mention of one inside a string literal, and a mention
            // is preceded by prose or by the quote that opens it — never by an
            // operator. So the test is the character before: a call follows
            // nothing, an arrow, a brace, an `=`, an open bracket, a comma or a
            // semicolon, and a mention follows a letter or a quote.
            // **And a method call sits on an expression.** `.map_err(` and
            // `.ok_or_else(` follow whatever produced the `Result` —
            // `read_to_string(path)`, `map[key]`, a name — where `.push(`'s
            // receiver is a plain path this rule already trims. A mention inside
            // a literal is still preceded by prose or by the quote that opens
            // it, which is what this test is for.
            let opens_a_call = before.is_empty()
                || before.ends_with("=>")
                || before.ends_with(['{', '}', '=', '(', ',', ';'])
                // **Parenthesised, because `&&` binds tighter than `||`.** Without
                // these brackets the alphanumeric test is a separate disjunct
                // applying to *every* needle — so "a mention follows a letter",
                // which the paragraph above gives as the whole reason this rule
                // exists, would be accepted. That cannot fire while the
                // `between` list is short, and goes live the moment the list
                // grows.
                || (matches!(needle, ".map_err(" | ".ok_or_else(")
                    && (before.ends_with([')', ']'])
                        || before
                            .chars()
                            .next_back()
                            .is_some_and(|last| last.is_alphanumeric() || last == '_')));
            if !opens_a_call {
                continue;
            }
        } else if before.ends_with("->") || before.ends_with("struct") {
            // A `Finding` is constructed anywhere — a helper's tail expression,
            // a `push` on any receiver, a `vec![]`, a match arm — so position
            // decides nothing here. What is not a construction is the type in a
            // signature and the type's own definition, and both are followed by
            // a `what` this would otherwise read as a sentence.
            continue;
        }
        let from = if field.is_empty() {
            at
        } else {
            at + text[at..]
                .find(field)
                .expect("a finding names the sentence it carries")
                + field.len()
        };
        let Some(offset) = text[from..].find('"') else {
            continue;
        };
        let quote = from + offset;
        // **The literal has to be the argument.** `ui::failure(&problem)` and
        // `return Err(other)` carry a value rather than a sentence, and taking
        // "the first quote after the call" then captures a literal from a
        // statement further down — measured, five such captures in the list.
        // What may stand between the call and its literal is a reference
        // and a `format!`, and nothing else.
        let between: String = text[from..quote]
            .chars()
            .filter(|character| !character.is_whitespace())
            .collect();
        // **And `concat!`, which is how a long verdict is written without a
        // wrap.** Measured without this, `ui::failure(concat!("a tree with no ",
        // "catalog is not this repository"))` is read by nothing.
        // **And `r"…"`, which is how a verdict holding a `\"` is written.**
        // The opener is neither a reference nor a `format!`: taken as it
        // stands, `between` is `r#`, which matches nothing here, so the
        // sentence would fall through every fallback unread.
        // `ui::warn(r#"skipped: …"#); return;` is `THE_SKIP`'s own subject in a
        // spelling that reading misses, and clippy does not object —
        // `needless_raw_string_hashes` is satisfied the moment the literal holds
        // a `"`, which is the whole reason to write one.
        // The wrappers come off by rule; see [`without_the_wrappers`].
        let unwrapped = without_the_wrappers(&between);
        let (stem, raw) = match the_raw_opener_in(unwrapped) {
            Some((before, hashes)) => (before, Some(hashes)),
            None => (unwrapped, None),
        };
        if !matches!(
            stem,
            "" | "&" | "format!(" | "&format!(" | "concat!(" | "&concat!("
                // **And `String::from`, which is `.to_owned()` written the other
                // way round.** The literal is the argument either way; only the
                // wrapper differs, and a wrapper is not a reason to stop reading.
                | "String::from(" | "&String::from("
        ) {
            found.extend(the_sentences_reached_indirectly(text, from, &regions));
            continue;
        }
        // A raw string ends at `"` followed by its own hashes, and it exists
        // to hold a `"` — so reading it to the next quote returns a fragment.
        let (sentence, length) = match raw {
            Some(hashes) => the_raw_literal_at(&text[quote..], hashes),
            None => the_literal_at(&text[quote..]),
        };
        // A `concat!` is one sentence written as several: every literal up to
        // the closing bracket belongs to it, which is what the macro means.
        // **A raw first piece does not stop it being a concatenation.** Written
        // `&& raw.is_none()`, one `r` in front of the first literal records that
        // fragment alone and leaves every later piece of the sentence free to be
        // reworded with the census green — the same shape as a `format!`
        // template with unpinned clauses.
        let (sentence, length) = if stem.ends_with("concat!(") {
            the_concatenation_at(&text[quote..], raw)
        } else {
            (sentence, length)
        };
        // **And what the message puts into itself.** The clause a verdict
        // chooses just above the call is the informative half of the line, and
        // the template around it is what this scan reads.
        for name in the_names_interpolated_into(&sentence) {
            found.extend(the_sentences_a_name_can_hold(&name, text, quote, &regions));
        }
        // **And a choice written as a later argument of the same call.** A scan
        // that reads the template and resumes past it leaves
        // `ui::detail(&format!("not readable as text: {}", if … { … } else { … }))`
        // — the line the marker census tells a reader what to do with — with a
        // pinned template and free clauses, following only a clause reached
        // through a *name* the template interpolates.
        // Bounded by the `format!` the template sits in: from the literal's
        // own start, the first bracket no other closes is that call's.
        if stem.ends_with("format!(")
            && let Some(end) = the_matching_bracket_of(&text[quote..])
        {
            let rest = quote + length;
            let closes = quote + end;
            if rest < closes && closes <= text.len() {
                found.extend(the_clauses_of(&text[rest..closes]));
            }
        }
        found.push(sentence);
        at = quote + length;
    }
    found
}

/// Sentences this tool ships that nobody has accounted for yet.
///
/// **A record of a real and large gap, not a rule that passes.** The census
/// above asks *where does a verdict start* and answers it by lexical adjacency:
/// from a needle it walks forward and accepts a short list of things that may
/// stand between the call and its quote. Four structural shapes no spelling
/// reaches — a closure passed to an iterator adaptor, a statement between the
/// brace and the verdict, a binder whose right-hand side is a call, and a
/// constructor that is no needle. Measured: **42 verdicts in 9 files reworded,
/// several inverted to state the opposite of what the code does, with
/// `cargo xtask check` exit 0 and the console byte-identical to baseline.**
///
/// Repairs that close one spelling at a time leave the class every time. So the
/// rule beside this list asks the opposite question — *is
/// every sentence this binary ships accounted for* — and it cannot be defeated
/// by a new expression shape, because it never looks at an expression.
///
/// These are sentences this tool ships that are in neither list.
/// They are not excused: they are the backlog.
///
/// **What holds it is an equality, not a ratchet.** The assertion beside the
/// list is `len() == THE_REST_IT_SAYS`, and the number goes *up* whenever this
/// tool learns to say something new — which is correct, because a new gate
/// ships new sentences and they have to be written down somewhere. A ratchet
/// would refuse them.
///
/// So: the length is pinned so that no row is appended in passing, and a
/// separate assertion refuses an entry this tool no longer ships. Neither
/// direction is free, and the count itself is written at the declaration rather
/// than here, because a count in a paragraph beside the list it counts is held
/// by nothing.
///
/// **The case names, which `THE_CASES` holds.** A case's name is the decision
/// it records, and the decision is what a total could not keep: one deleted is
/// paid for by another written in its place. Freezing the set puts every case's
/// name into this crate's source, and this census's rule is that a sentence a
/// reader can see is a sentence some list holds — so a case added under
/// `policy/` is a row here as well as one in `THE_CASES`.
///
/// **Written here rather than between the rows.** The array is kept in order,
/// and prose between sorted rows is prose a sort may reorder — a line-wise sort
/// sweeps a paragraph's lines apart and leaves them alphabetised, a
/// `// **The forty-one` line followed by four beginning `is`, `one`, `put` and
/// `the`. Prose above the rows is not reordered.
#[cfg(test)]
